he issue  since last week's testimony from a  former senior advisor revealed the prime  minister's chief of staff  knew about the allegation and knew it  involved personal misconduct  documents obtained by global news show  senior advisors were told the allegation  involved sexual harassment  as mike lucatura reports they also  challenged trudeau's suggestion today  that his office didn't know the details  nobody knew that it was a  me too complaint prime minister justin  trudeau again  insisted neither he his defense minister  nor his staff  knew the nature of a 2018 allegation  against former chief of the defense  staff general jonathan vance however  last week  elder marquez a former senior adviser to  the prime minister  told the commons committee he had an  inkling my  presumption was certainly that it could  be of a sexual nature and i  but i don't think i was actually given  that information specifically an email  from a senior bureaucrat at the time to  a redacted individual referenced quote  allegations of sexual harassment flagged  by the military ombudsman  the previous day the bureaucrat said at  committee the redacted individuals were  people in the prime minister's office  will the prime minister be honest with  this house  and with the women serving bravely in  our canadian armed forces  that he was aware and he failed them for  three years in response trudeau repeated  his government took the appropriate  actions when they found out about the  allegation  in 2015 when a complaint complaint came  forward  we forwarded that to the the privy  council office so that they  could do the follow-up necessary but  unfortunately the ombudsman  was not able to reveal the full extent  because he didn't have permission  but there remain questions about who  knew what  marquez testified last week trudeau's  chief of staff katie telford  was in the loop on the file tuesday  trudeau leapt  to her defense katie's leadership on  this every step of the way  is why we call ourselves a famous  feminist  government and why we will continue to  look to do more the government has  promised an independent investigation  into the allegations of sexual  misconduct in the military and three  months after our reporting  first brought this to light senior  sources tell global news  an announcement is expected within the  next week or two  mike lacouter global news ottawa  caught on camera coming up the violent  arrest of a woman with dementia and how  police  joked about it  [Music]  there's another troubling story about  police use of force this one involving a  woman with dementia  the 73 year old colorado woman was  forcibly arrested last june  karen garner wandered out of a walmart  store without paying for about 13  dollars worth of goods  the police body cam footage of her  arrest  is disturbing  an officer grabs her arms pushes her to  the ground and handcuffs her  she appears distressed and repeatedly  says she is going home  her lawyer says her shoulder was  dislocated her arm fractured  and her wrist sprained stand up i'm  not doing anything right now you're  resisting  later in this video from inside the  police station officers congratulate  each other on the arrest and laugh about  it as they review the video  one officer says here comes the pop as  the woman's shoulder  is dislocated karen garner's family is  suing the police alleging excessive  force and  accusing them of holding her for six  hours without medical help  they say her dementia has since worsened  one officer has been put on  administrative leave two others moved to  administrative duties  and a criminal investigation is underway  tax time the complications of filing  during a pandemic  friday's deadline for filing your taxes  is looming  new options new rules and new  circumstances mean millions of canadians  are faced with a more complicated tax  return this year  if you owe and don't file on time not  only could you be penalized there may be  a delay in receiving other much needed  benefits  as our senior business correspondent ann  gaviola explains  how and when you file is more important  than ever  2020 was a big adjustment for amanda  soriano the pandemic took a toll on her  wedding photography side business and  her full-time job became remote  i was excited for the first two weeks  and then as time went by i was kind of  missing the day-to-day interactions with  colleagues and essentially leaving the  house  ig wealth management says nearly half of  filers are making claims related to  working remotely this year  for many that's the new 400 work from  home tax break  so as long as you were working from home  for fifty percent of the time  um since last march two dollars a day  uh up to two hundred days so you can get  four hundred dollars in total  uh you can deduct that amount covid 19  relief benefits are a complicating  factor  impacting about 45 percent of filings  this year tax experts say many  recipients who owe money  haven't budgeted for it there's some  relief if your total taxable income in  2020 was 75 000  or less and you received at least one  covet 19 benefit  you automatically have a grace period on  interest for one year  but you still have to file by friday and  missing the deadline could delay a host  of other government payments from the  canada child benefit to tax credits  and recovery benefits accountants are  under pressure too  especially at those smaller firms  struggling to keep up with new rules and  more paperwork  chartered professional accountants have  asked the federal government to extend  the deadline like they did last year but  ottawa said no  clients are having trouble getting  information to them  and they're doing a lot more work as  well because they've been helping  their clients apply for the wage and  rent subsidy and some of the other  assistance programs as well  so it's been a very difficult time for  them how you file is important too  last year 90 of filers did so online and  the cra says processing times for  electronic filings are three to five  days compared with 10 to 12  weeks for paper returns so if you can  file online it is recommended  ann gaviola global news toronto  up next how a love of literature helped  three women  write off the pandemic  during the first two waves of this  pandemic few places were more vulnerable  than long-term care homes  thousands of residents got sick and died  and those who survived endured months of  lockdown  well three women who live in long-term  care in calgary found a way to get  through  by turning to each other and their love  of literature  heather urex west has their story  at long-term care facilities around the  world  covid19 is having a devastating impact  from the outside the suffering and loss  of life is difficult to watch  for those living inside it's even harder  we couldn't see anybody it couldn't go  anywhere diana van giffen heather graham  and loretta scrotchings are residents of  care west garrison green  in calgary a long-term care facility  that accommodates  seniors and younger adults with  disabilities like them  graham's lived here for eight years she  has  ms but a sharp mind and an active social  circle  that is until kovit 19 shut everything  down  at certain times there were some of the  staff that had come down with kovid  and so we had to be all tested and then  we had to stay in our rooms for 14 days  cut off from family and friends and  unable to leave the women took comfort  in each other  and a shared love of books from our book  club  we had an idea that well we would like  to try writing the book  with the help of a recreational  therapist the trio began writing  together in  2018 unaware that the fictional world  they were creating would become  a place of refuge and escape from the  frightening reality  that would surround them soon i really  think it did help us with the pandemic  because  like we we had something to focus  ourselves on  it's why book three written in 2020  is a lot longer than the previous tales  it started with one line  and then we just kept going  the mysteries set in a fictional town  near prince rupert bc  are now available on amazon but the  women hope the trilogy  tells another story too it gave us a  chance to show that we're not just  wasting away here  long-term care isn't the end and you  still you get to live  vaccinated now and ready to see what  their next  chapters will bring heather eurex west  global news  calgary and that is global national for  this tuesday i'm donna friesen  tonight's your canada is one for the  books it's the first super moon of 2021  it's called a pink super moon  named after the pink phlox wildflower a  full moon won't be this close to the  earth and this bright for another month  so try to get a look at it if you can  thanks for watching  take care of yourselves bye  [Music]  you
 

